{
  "title": "Make the database layer generic and pilot SQLite in place of Postgres",
  "description": "Owner direction: genericize the database access and probably pilot moving from Postgres to SQLite, unless pre-analysis shows that would wreck performance. \
Related: blocked-ramekin-mcp-chatgpt mentions running the whole app inside Cloudflare on SQLite as one option. \
Step 1, pre-analysis for the owner before any migration work: inventory Postgres-specific features in use (pg_current_xact_id()/xid8 change_xid cursors and their triggers, jsonb columns and operators, uuid, timestamptz, ON CONFLICT clauses, array types, eq_any, raw SQL in server/src/raw_sql.rs and migrations, full-text or trigram search if any, advisory locks, connection-pool concurrency assumptions like the background workers writing while requests read). \
For each, say what the SQLite equivalent is or what has to change; the xid sync cursors in particular need a replacement (see blocked-one-sync-mechanism). \
Estimate performance with realistic data sizes (the owner's prod recipe count, shopping lists, step_outputs, ingredient-name tables) for the hot paths: recipe list/search, sync, scrape job writes alongside reads (SQLite allows one writer at a time; check WAL mode and how long the heaviest write transactions are, such as data migrations and queue_stored). \
Recommend go/no-go. \
Step 2 if go: put database access behind Diesel's backend-generic code where practical (MultiConnection or a backend type parameter), keep Postgres working, add SQLite as a second backend, and run the e2e suite against both. \
Preserve soft-delete semantics and all existing data; write the data migration plan before switching prod.",
